# Normalise line endings: the repository stores LF, Windows checkouts may use
# CRLF locally. Files consumed by Linux containers are pinned to LF so that
# they remain executable/parsable inside Docker regardless of host platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.ts            text eol=lf
*.tsx           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be line-ending converted.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary

# Audio fixtures (Phase 4). `*.pcm` matters most: headerless PCM would
# otherwise fall under `* text=auto`, leaving git's binary heuristic to decide
# whether to rewrite CRLF inside sample data. The heuristic usually gets it
# right, which is exactly what makes the failure rare and hard to diagnose -
# a corrupted fixture only looks like a mysteriously failing audio test.
*.pcm  binary
*.raw  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.flac binary
*.aac  binary
*.m4a  binary
